# bstar site settings. On the server this file lives in the PARAMS branch,
# not beside the scripts:
#
#   cp bstar.env.example /datalakebin/prod/gold/integration/params/bluestar/bstar.env
#   chmod 600 .../params/bluestar/bstar.env
#
# Because it is not beside the launcher, EVERY invocation (interactive or
# Control-M) must export its location first:
#
#   export BSTAR_ENV_FILE=/datalakebin/prod/gold/integration/params/bluestar/bstar.env
#
# bstar.env is gitignored: it holds site values, not framework configuration.
# HOW the feeds behave lives in the .conf files; only WHERE they run lives
# here. There is NO credential in this file and none is needed: the hive
# source reads through the Spark session's own metastore/HDFS access.

# ---- Databases --------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION: leave all three UNSET. The feeds default to
#   source  bstar_raw          (read-only, owned by the Sqoop landing)
#   control bluestar_raw       (ledger / rejects / watermarks / locks ONLY)
#   curated bluestar_current   (consumer-facing)
# and run_bstar.sh prints a NOTE for every override it finds, because an
# override that is set by accident points production at the wrong database
# without any error.
#BSTAR_SOURCE_DB=bstar_raw
#BSTAR_CONTROL_DB=bluestar_raw
#BSTAR_CURATED_DB=bluestar_current

# LOWER ENVIRONMENT (run_bstar.sh e2e ...): one database holds the synthetic
# source tables, the curated tables and the control tables. Default
# bluestar_e2e; must match the database in lower-env/ddl/source_test_data.sql.
#BSTAR_E2E_DB=bluestar_e2e

# ---- Site paths (DEPLOYMENT.md "Site layout") --------------------------------
# Directory holding feed-bstar-*.conf and the included bstar-*-schema.conf,
# side by side (both the prod and the e2e pairs can live here together).
BSTAR_CONF_DIR=/datalakebin/prod/gold/integration/params/bluestar

# Assembly jar built from main. The same jar serves every feed.
BSTAR_JAR=/datalakebin/prod/gold/integration/bin/bluestar/ingestion-app-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT-jar-with-dependencies.jar

# ---- Submit -----------------------------------------------------------------
# client  = driver runs here; env inherited; logs on your terminal.
# cluster = driver runs in a YARN container; what Control-M uses.
BSTAR_DEPLOY_MODE=client

# Driver heap — the contracts are narrow (24 / 30 columns), so the plan is
# small; 4g is comfortable headroom for the merge.
INGEST_DRIVER_MEMORY=4g
# Executor heap — sub_prem_det first light parses ~30 GB of uncompressed
# pipe-delimited text. Set for that run; the daily increments do not need it.
#INGEST_EXECUTOR_MEMORY=8g

# ---- Alerting ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The feed configs reference ${?BSTAR_ALERT_WEBHOOK}. Unset = the placeholder
# in the feed applies and delivery fails (logged, run unaffected).
#BSTAR_ALERT_WEBHOOK=https://hooks.example.com/services/XXXX

# ---- Freshness checks (Control-M AUDIT folder) ------------------------------
# check_freshness.sh (ledger) and check_source_freshness.sh (source
# partitions) read these. The audit database is the CONTROL database.
#INGEST_HIVE_JDBC=jdbc:hive2://hiveserver:10000/default;principal=hive/_HOST@REALM
#INGEST_AUDIT_DB=bluestar_raw

# ---- Operational override (optional) ----------------------------------------
# A second config whose values WIN over the feed config, for changes that
# cannot wait for a change order — a longer lease, a wider lookback, a
# relaxed reject threshold. See params/override-bstar.conf.example.
# Objects merge, lists are replaced. Every overridden path is logged by
# name; the ledger marks the run with an "+ovr:" fingerprint suffix. A path
# set here but missing on disk FAILS the run (CFG_019). Remove it once the
# real change is deployed.
#INGEST_OVERRIDE_FILE=/datalakebin/prod/gold/integration/params/bluestar/override-bstar.conf
